Dutch sailors landed on Mauritius in 1598. They wrote that dodos were tough to chew. They also noted that the birds were unafraid and easy to catch. Ships began stopping there for fresh water and food.

The Dutch set up a settlement in 1638. They brought pigs, goats and dogs, and rats came ashore from the ships. Crab-eating monkeys were also brought to the island. Dodos laid a single egg on the ground, which made them easy targets. The last widely accepted sighting was in 1662. Some scientists think a few may have survived until around 1690.

{{story:mughal-miniatures}}

A newly hatched sunfish is only about 2.5 mm long, smaller than a grain of rice and covered in tiny spines. By adulthood, some grow around 60 million times heavier. No other backboned animal is known to grow so much.

A big female may carry hundreds of millions of eggs. One female measured in the early 1900s was estimated to hold about 300 million. Almost all the babies are eaten by other animals, which is why the seas are not packed with sunfish.

{{story:animal-records}}

The deeper you go, the more water presses down from above. Every 10 m of depth adds about the same pressure again as the whole weight of the air above us. At 300 m, the water presses about 30 times harder than the air at the surface.

So submarines are built with a strong inner hull, usually round in cross-section, because a round shape spreads the squeeze evenly. Military submarines usually keep exact diving depths secret. Most travel a few hundred metres down. To reach the deepest parts of the sea, you need a much smaller, tougher vessel called a submersible, as you can read in [[@ocean-deepest|the deepest ocean]].

{{story:american-revolution}}

Propane and butane have a handy trick. Push them under moderate pressure and they turn into a liquid, taking up about 250 times less space. That is why one cylinder can last a family a month or more.

When you turn the knob, pressure inside drops a little, and some of the liquid boils back into gas. It flows through the rubber pipe to the burner. If you shake a cylinder gently, you can hear the liquid slosh. A full Indian home cylinder holds 14.2 kg of LPG, and the steel cylinder itself weighs about as much again. An empty cylinder still holds a little gas, which is why it never feels completely light.

{{story:fire-burns}}

जब हज़ारों रँगे धागे करघे पर चढ़ते हैं, तो हर एक थोड़ा-सा हिलता है। रंग पूरी तरह एक सीध में नहीं आते। इसलिए इकत के नमूनों के किनारे नरम और झिलमिलाते होते हैं, मानो तपती दोपहर में सड़क पर उठती लहरों के पार से देखे गए हों।

बुनकर नमूने को सीध में लाने के लिए हर धागे को हाथ से ठीक करते हैं। धुँधलापन गलती नहीं है। इसी से लोग असली इकत पहचानते हैं। बहुत-से बुनकर पहले ग्राफ़ पेपर पर डिज़ाइन बनाते हैं और खानों में वैसे ही रंग भरते हैं जैसे स्क्रीन [[pixel|पिक्सेल]] जलाती है।

{{story:pixels}}

In World War II, the German forces used a machine called Enigma. Its spinning wheels changed the cipher after every single letter typed, and the settings changed every day.

Polish mathematicians broke an early version in 1932 and shared their work with Britain just before the war began. At Bletchley Park in England, thousands of people worked in secret, including the mathematician [[@alan-turing|Alan Turing]]. They built electric machines to test settings quickly. Their work stayed secret until the 1970s.

{{story:alan-turing}}

Around 2002, the Oakland Athletics, a baseball team with far less money than its rivals, began picking players using [[statistics|statistics]] instead of scouts' hunches. They found that some skills, like getting on base, were undervalued and cheap to buy. The team won many games on a small budget, and the writer Michael Lewis told the story in his book *Moneyball*.

[[@basketball|Basketball]] went through its own number revolution. Analysts pointed out that a three-point shot, even if it goes in less often, can be worth more on average than a two-pointer. In recent years, [[@nba|teams in the NBA]] have taken far more threes than teams did in the 1990s.

{{story:sports-science}}

Roll two dice and add them up. The totals run from 2 to 12, but they aren't equally likely. There is only one way to make 2: a one and a one. There is only one way to make 12.

Seven can be made in six ways: 1 and 6, 2 and 5, 3 and 4, and each of those the other way round. Out of 36 possible pairs, six make seven, so it comes up one time in six. That is why seven turns up so often in games played with two dice. Many board games, including most versions of [[@snakes-ladders|Snakes and Ladders]], use a single dice instead, where every number from 1 to 6 is equally likely.

{{story:snakes-ladders}}

In 1912 he got a job as a clerk at the Madras Port Trust, in today's [[@chennai|Chennai]]. Friends and officers there encouraged him to send his work abroad. In January 1913, he wrote to G. H. Hardy and enclosed pages packed with formulas.

Hardy at first suspected it might be a joke. Then he and his colleague J. E. Littlewood studied it. Some results were already known, some were wrong, and some were entirely new and astonishing. Hardy later wrote that a few of them must be true, because no one would have the imagination to invent them. He arranged for Ramanujan to come to Cambridge.

{{story:chennai}}

Astronauts on the [[@space-station|International Space Station]] float around, so it seems as if they have no weight. But the station is only about 400 km up. Gravity there is still about 90% as strong as on the ground.

The station and everyone in it are falling towards the Earth all the time. They are also moving sideways at about 28,000 km/h, so fast that they keep falling around the planet instead of into it. Because everything falls together, nothing presses on anything else. That is why astronauts feel weightless.

{{story:astronaut-life}}

In India, archery is woven into old stories. In the [[@mahabharata|Mahabharata]], the teacher Drona tests his students by asking them to shoot a wooden bird in a tree. Only Arjuna says he can see nothing but the bird's eye. It is still a famous lesson in focus.

Another story is about Ekalavya, a tribal boy refused as a student. He taught himself by practising in front of a clay statue of Drona and became a brilliant archer. The story raises questions about fairness that people still discuss.

{{story:springs}}

India first played Olympic hockey at Amsterdam in 1928. The team did not let in a single goal all [[tournament|tournament]], and Dhyan Chand was the top scorer. India beat the Netherlands 3–0 in the final. When the team sailed home, crowds met them at the docks in Bombay. Only a few people had seen them off.

In 1932 the Games were held in [[@los-angeles|Los Angeles]]. Few teams could afford the journey, so only three took part. India beat Japan 11–1 and the USA 24–1. Dhyan Chand's younger brother, Roop Singh, scored about as many goals as he did.

{{story:hockey-india}}

Coubertin wanted sport to build healthy young people and bring nations together. The first modern Games, in Athens in 1896, had about 240 athletes, all men, from 14 countries. Winners did not get gold medals. They got a silver medal and an olive branch. Women first competed in 1900, in Paris.

Many traditions are newer than people think. [[@pop-olympic-torch|The Olympic flame]] was first lit at the 1928 Games in Amsterdam. The torch relay from Olympia began in 1936.

{{story:fastest-animal}}

By the time a signal has travelled 36,000 km, it is very weak. The dish works like a cupped hand behind your ear. Its curved shape reflects the signal and focuses it on one small point, where a part called the LNB sits on an arm in front of the dish.

The LNB boosts the signal and passes it down a cable to the set-top box. The box decodes the channels and checks whether you have paid for them. Bigger dishes catch more signal, which is why the dishes of the 1980s were often wider than a person was tall.

{{story:science-fiction}}

Seeing is half the job. A car must also know exactly where it is. [[@gps-how|GPS]] can be off by a few metres, which is the difference between your lane and the wrong side of the road.

So many self-driving cars carry detailed maps made in advance, showing every lane, kerb and traffic light. As the car drives, it compares what its sensors see with the map, like you checking street signs against directions. That way it can place itself to within a few centimetres.

{{story:gps-how}}

In the early 1990s, a team at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in California wanted smaller, lighter cameras for spacecraft. Engineer Eric Fossum and his colleagues developed a new kind of image sensor that put the light-catching parts and the electronics on one cheap chip. It also used far less power than older sensors.

The idea spread into webcams, then into phones. Today almost every [[@cameras-phones|phone camera]] uses this kind of sensor, which works in much the same way as the ones in [[@cameras-how|cameras]] that fly on spacecraft. A selfie in Kolkata or Kansas City owes something to a design meant for Mars.

{{story:satellites-help}}

Chinese printers carved a whole page of text backwards into a wooden block, inked it, and pressed paper onto it. The Diamond Sutra of 868 CE, found in a cave at Dunhuang, is the oldest printed book that carries a date.

In the 1040s, a craftsman named Bi Sheng made movable type: single characters baked from clay that could be arranged into any page and reused. Chinese has thousands of characters, so wooden blocks often remained more practical. In Europe, with a small alphabet, [[@printing-revolution|Gutenberg]]'s [[@printing-press|printing press]] of the 1450s made movable type a huge success.

{{story:printing-press}}

Each type of gladiator had strengths and weaknesses, and they were paired to make a fair fight. The *retiarius* fought almost unarmoured, with a net and a trident, like a fisherman. He was fast. His usual opponent, the *secutor*, wore a smooth helmet with tiny eyeholes so the net couldn't catch it. He was protected but tired quickly.

The *murmillo* had a big shield and a fish-shaped crest. The *thraex* carried a small shield and a curved sword. Referees watched the bouts and could stop a fight if a gladiator raised a finger to give up.

{{story:colosseum}}

In 1964, Shastri visited the dairy [[cooperative|cooperative]] in Anand, Gujarat, that made [[@amul|Amul]] butter and milk. He was so impressed by how village farmers ran it together that he wanted the idea copied across India. In 1965 his government set up the National Dairy Development Board, led by Verghese Kurien.

That decision helped launch the [[@white-revolution|White Revolution]], which turned India into the world's biggest milk producer.

{{story:amul}}

In 1897, ships arrived in Seattle carrying gold from the Klondike, in northern Canada. News spread fast, and tens of thousands of people passed through Seattle on their way north to join the [[@gold-rush|gold rush]]. Shops selling supplies made the city rich.

In 1916, William Boeing founded an [[@made-planes|aircraft company]] in Seattle. It became one of the world's biggest makers of planes, and helped the city grow during the 20th century. Later, technology companies, including [[@sundar-ganges-tech|Microsoft]] and Amazon, made the Seattle area a major tech centre. Starbucks opened its first coffee shop beside Pike Place Market in 1971, and Seattle became famous for its coffee culture.

{{story:floating-ships}}

In 1791, a French-born engineer, Pierre Charles L'Enfant, drew a plan with wide avenues spreading out from the Capitol and the President's House like spokes on a wheel. Surveyor Andrew Ellicott and the [[astronomer|astronomer]] Benjamin Banneker, a free Black man, helped mark out the boundaries.

During the War of 1812, British troops burned the Capitol and the [[@white-house|White House]] in 1814. Both were rebuilt. The President's House was painted white, and by the early 1900s everyone called it the White House, which became its official name in 1901. The Capitol's great cast-iron dome was added in the 1850s and 1860s. Work on it carried on even during the [[@us-civil-war|Civil War]], because President Abraham Lincoln said it was a sign that the nation would go on.

{{story:congress-usa}}

कोको का पेड़ [[equator|भूमध्य रेखा]] के पास गर्म और गीली जगहों पर, ऊँचे पेड़ों की छाँव में उगता है। इसकी फलियाँ सीधे तने से फूटती हैं।

कोको अमेज़न के ऊपरी इलाक़े से आया है। वैज्ञानिकों को इक्वाडोर में लगभग 5,300 साल पुराने बर्तनों में इसके निशान मिले। बाद में [[@mexico|मेक्सिको]] और मध्य अमेरिका के ओल्मेक, [[@aztecs-maya|माया और एज़्टेक]] लोग कोको को एक कड़वे, झागदार पेय की तरह पीते थे, अक्सर उसमें मिर्च डालकर। एज़्टेक लोग तो कोको के बीजों को पैसे की तरह भी इस्तेमाल करते थे।

{{story:aztecs-maya}}

केले का गुच्छा एक विशाल बैंगनी कली से शुरू होता है, जो लंबी डंडी पर नीचे झुकी रहती है। उसकी पंखुड़ियों के नीचे छोटे फूलों की क़तारें खुलती हैं। हर मादा फूल के पीछे एक नन्हा हरा केला होता है।

शुरू में ये नन्ही उँगलियाँ नीचे की ओर होती हैं, क्योंकि पूरी डंडी लटकी होती है। फिर कुछ हफ़्तों में वे मुड़कर ऊपर बढ़ने लगती हैं। हर फल के निचले हिस्से की [[cell|कोशिकाएँ]] ऊपर वाली कोशिकाओं से तेज़ बढ़ती हैं। इससे केला मुड़ जाता है, ठीक वैसे ही जैसे एक तरफ़ ज़्यादा बढ़ने से कोई अंकुर खिड़की की ओर झुक जाता है। यह उन कई धीमे तरीक़ों में से एक है जिनसे [[@plants-move|पौधे बिना मांसपेशियों के हिलते-डुलते हैं]]।

{{story:plants-move}}
